# Copy to .env (gitignored) and fill in.
#
# THE STACK BOOTS WITH NO .env AT ALL. Every key docker-compose.yml reads carries a working
# default, so this file is not a checklist you have to complete before your first `up` — it is the
# short list of what you SHOULD set before the instance holds anything real, plus the handful of
# values that decide where it is reachable and which release it runs.
#
# It is deliberately small. Every optional integration — WorkOS SSO, the GitHub App's deploy-time
# credentials, Slack, the encoder classify-service, the E2B launcher, Grafana Cloud and Langfuse —
# is configured in the running app or documented in docs/self-hosting/configuration.mdx rather
# than listed here, because a key you must rule out is a key you had to read.
# devdocs/reference/config-keys.md is the exhaustive prefix→class map: every
# @ConfigurationProperties field is env-settable through Spring relaxed binding
# (tessary.redaction.enabled → TESSARY_REDACTION_ENABLED) whether or not it appears in either file.
#
# MODEL PROVIDER KEYS ARE NOT HERE AND NEVER WILL BE. Credentials are org-scoped and entered once
# in the running app under Settings → Providers, sealed with TESSARY_SECRET_KEY below.
#
# ONE-COMMAND INSTALL (no clone): a .env in the directory you run
#   docker compose -f oci://docker.io/tessaryai/tessary:compose up -d -y
# from still sets every key docker-compose.yml names as a ${VAR} — which is every key in this
# file. It does NOT reach keys that are passthrough-only: the published artifact carries no
# env_file, because including one would ship the PUBLISHER's .env to everyone. Those need a small
# override file instead; docs/self-hosting/setup.mdx's "Operating a stack you started from a
# registry" has the recipe.

# ============================================================================
# The three keys to set before this instance holds anything real
# ============================================================================
#
# All three are COMMENTED OUT ON PURPOSE. docker-compose.yml carries a placeholder default for
# each so the stack boots with no .env at all, and that file is the ONE place the literal is
# written down — repeating it here would give two files that can drift, with compose's
# `environment:` silently winning. Read the values there.
#
# The placeholders are public. An instance running them is not weakly configured, it is READABLE:
# anyone can decrypt every credential it has sealed, anyone can forge a session cookie against it,
# and anyone can drive its sandbox launcher into spawning containers on the host, all with no prior
# access to anything. Generate your own with:
#
#   openssl rand -base64 32
#
# Set SITE_DOMAIN with any of the three placeholders still in place and the backend refuses to
# start (PlaceholderSecretGuard) rather than serving a reachable host on a key published in our own
# repository. That guard is the line between a localhost test drive and a real deployment.

# AES-256-GCM key (SecretBox) sealing everything the platform stores on a user's behalf:
# ingestion-source credentials, the per-project provider API keys entered under Settings →
# Providers, and the signed GitHub install-state token. Rotating it invalidates everything already
# sealed, so commit to one value per environment and keep it with your database backups — a
# pg_dump restored without this key cannot be decrypted.
# TESSARY_SECRET_KEY=

# Base64 of 32 random bytes (AES-256 cookie key). Provider-agnostic: every AuthProvider
# adapter shares one session cookie, so it lives under TESSARY_AUTH_* rather than WORKOS_*.
# TESSARY_AUTH_COOKIE_PASSWORD=

# The bearer the backend presents to the sandbox launcher, and the launcher checks it against.
# Compose defaults both halves to one placeholder so RCA and triage runs work out of the box;
# anyone holding that published value can drive the launcher into spawning containers on the host.
# TESSARY_OBSERVER_AGENTIC_LAUNCHER_API_KEY=

# ============================================================================
# Database
# ============================================================================
#
# Postgres is REQUIRED — there is no embedded fallback. The schema needs pgvector, so point at a
# pgvector-enabled Postgres (the shipped image is pgvector/pgvector:pg16). Compose constructs
# TESSARY_JDBC_URL from the three values below; you never set that URL yourself for a compose stack.
#
# All three default to `tessary`, so an instance boots with none of them set. The postgres
# service publishes no host port, so the credential is reachable only from the compose network —
# change the password if you publish that port or share the host.
POSTGRES_DB=tessary
POSTGRES_USER=tessary
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=tessary

# What backs the Postgres data volume. Defaults to the NAMED VOLUME tessary-postgres-data, so a
# fresh install leaves nothing on disk that Docker does not manage. Set it to a host PATH to bind
# one instead — compose decides by the value's shape. `docker compose down` keeps the volume;
# `down -v` deletes the data.
# POSTGRES_DATA_DIR=/data/postgres

# ============================================================================
# Where this instance is reachable
# ============================================================================
#
# SITE_DOMAIN is the line between a localhost test drive and a real deployment, and the ONE
# variable that puts Tessary on your hostname (docs/self-hosting/custom-domain.mdx). A bare
# hostname, no scheme: tessary.acme-corp.com. Set it and the sign-in landing origin, the WorkOS
# callback and the agentic lanes' callback origin are all derived from it, the placeholder-key
# guard above arms, and Caddy serves it the way TLS_MODE says. Leave it blank for plain HTTP on
# HTTP_PORT.
# Optional, leave blank.
SITE_DOMAIN=

# How SITE_DOMAIN is served:
#   acme      Caddy obtains a Let's Encrypt certificate over TLS-ALPN-01. Needs HTTPS_PORT (443)
#             reachable from the internet and ACME_EMAIL set. No DNS provider, no Cloudflare token.
#   owncert   Your own certificate and key, mounted at /certs/tls.crt and /certs/tls.key in the
#             frontend container (an internal CA, a purchased wildcard). No ACME attempt.
#   upstream  A TLS terminator you already run sits in front; Caddy serves plain HTTP on HTTP_PORT
#             and honours the forwarded client address and scheme from TRUSTED_PROXIES only.
TLS_MODE=acme

# The Let's Encrypt account address, yours: expiry and revocation notices go to it. Required when
# SITE_DOMAIN is set and TLS_MODE is acme; the backend refuses to start without it rather than
# register the account to someone else's mailbox.
# Optional, leave blank unless TLS_MODE is acme with a SITE_DOMAIN.
ACME_EMAIL=

# Space-separated CIDRs of the proxy or load balancer in front of Tessary, for TLS_MODE=upstream
# (10.0.0.0/8, or a single /32). Forwarded headers from any other peer are ignored.
# Optional, leave blank unless TLS_MODE is upstream.
TRUSTED_PROXIES=

# Host ports → the frontend container. Default 80 and 443; bump HTTP_PORT (e.g. 8001) when running
# this stack alongside something else on the host.
# HTTP_PORT=80
# HTTPS_PORT=443

# ============================================================================
# Ingest buffer
# ============================================================================
#
# What holds an accepted batch between the OTLP edge and the database. `memory` (the default) needs
# no extra service: a 200 means queued in this process, the buffer sheds with 503 + Retry-After when
# full, and a restart loses what was queued. `kafka` means a 200 is a persisted publish to a Kafka-API
# broker and nothing acknowledged is lost on restart: start the bundled single-node Redpanda with
# `docker compose --profile kafka up -d`, or point TESSARY_INGEST_SPOOL_KAFKA_BOOTSTRAP_SERVERS at a
# broker you already run. docs/self-hosting/configuration.mdx, "Ingest buffer".
TESSARY_INGEST_SPOOL_MODE=memory
# Optional, leave blank unless TESSARY_INGEST_SPOOL_MODE is kafka and the broker is not the bundled one.
TESSARY_INGEST_SPOOL_KAFKA_BOOTSTRAP_SERVERS=

# ============================================================================
# Data retention
# ============================================================================
#
# How long the instance keeps what it ingests, in days; an hourly sweep deletes what is older.
# 0 means keep forever. These are the install-wide defaults; a project can override either one
# under Settings → Data retention (docs/self-hosting/configuration.mdx, "Data retention").
TESSARY_RETENTION_TRACE_TTL_DAYS=90
TESSARY_RETENTION_DETECTION_TTL_DAYS=90

# ============================================================================
# Telemetry (home.tessary.ai heartbeat)
# ============================================================================
#
# One anonymous, opt-OUT heartbeat ping: install id and coarse bucketed counts, never trace content
# and never identities. ON by default; set it to false for zero outbound calls — including
# DNS — to that host. docs/self-hosting/configuration.mdx's Telemetry section explains what it
# sends and what turning it off costs; devdocs/reference/telemetry-contract.md is the
# field-by-field wire contract for auditing the payload against the running code.
TESSARY_TELEMETRY_ENABLED=true

# ============================================================================
# Which release this stack runs
# ============================================================================
#
# TESSARY_VERSION moves all four images at once: one repo on Docker Hub, per-service version
# tags. Set one of the per-service keys directly to pin just that service to something else (a
# different version, a local build tag). No personal namespace is ever named here.
#
# UNSET MEANS `-latest`. From a clone, leaving TESSARY_VERSION out tracks the newest release; a
# `docker compose pull` then moves the stack forward. Write a version below to stop that. The
# published one-command install is unaffected either way — the release stamps its own version into
# the compose artifact, so `oci://docker.io/tessaryai/tessary:compose` is already pinned.
#
# The version to write is a released git tag with its `v` stripped: `git tag --list 'v*'` in a
# clone, or the newest release on the repository's releases page. Nothing in this repository
# hard-codes one, deliberately — the tag is the only source of truth, so there is no second copy
# to go stale. Replace <version> below.
#
# (No compose-artifact key: the one-command install pins its version in the reference itself —
# `oci://docker.io/tessaryai/tessary:compose-<version>` — because the artifact IS the file that
# would otherwise read the key.)
# TESSARY_VERSION=<version>
# BACKEND_IMAGE=tessaryai/tessary:backend-<version>
# FRONTEND_IMAGE=tessaryai/tessary:frontend-<version>
# SANDBOX_RUNNER_IMAGE=tessaryai/tessary:sandbox-runner-<version>

# The agent image each sandboxed RCA/triage run spawns as a sibling container. Moves with
# TESSARY_VERSION above; pin it alone only to run an image you built yourself. Not published until
# a human dispatches .github/workflows/release.yml for the first time — "Run RCA" 502s until then.
# AGENT_IMAGE=tessaryai/tessary:agent-sandbox-<version>
#
# The same agent runtime, for the OTHER backend. Only read when SANDBOX_BACKEND=e2b, and only
# useful alongside your own E2B_API_KEY. The template is public, so your key can resolve it; the
# `tessary/` prefix is the E2B project that publishes it and is not optional — a bare name resolves
# only inside that project. Unset floats to `:latest`, which each release repoints.
# E2B_ANALYZER_TEMPLATE=tessary/tessary-agent-sandbox:<version>

# The GID granting the launcher container write access to the mounted /var/run/docker.sock. Read it
# as a container sees the socket:
#   docker run --rm -v /var/run/docker.sock:/var/run/docker.sock busybox:1.36 stat -c %g /var/run/docker.sock
# 999 is a common Debian/Ubuntu default; Docker Desktop mounts the socket root:root, so there the
# answer is 0. Wrong value ⇒ the launcher starts and every "Run RCA" fails on a permission error.
# DOCKER_SOCK_GID=999
